```html
<style>
  :root { --surface: #f7f8fb; --ink: #172033; }
  .notice { --surface: #fff4d6; }
  .card { background: var(--surface, white); color: var(--ink, black); }
</style>
<article class="card">Default card</article>
<section class="notice"><article class="card">Warning card</article></section>
```
Both cards use one component rule. The second inherits the nearer --surface override.

```html
<style>
  :root { --accent: #2458d3; }
  .danger-zone { --accent: #a83232; }
  .action { border: 2px solid var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
</style>
<button class="action">Save</button>
<div class="danger-zone"><button class="action">Delete</button></div>
```
The second button receives red through its parent; the reusable .action rule does not change.

```html
<style>
  :root { color-scheme: light dark; --surface: #ffffff; --text: #172033; }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root { --surface: #172033; --text: #f5f7fb; }
  }
  :root[data-theme="light"] { --surface: #ffffff; --text: #172033; }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] { --surface: #172033; --text: #f5f7fb; }
  .card { background: var(--surface); color: var(--text); }
</style>
<article class="card">Theme-aware content</article>
```
An explicit data-theme value overrides the automatic pair because its rule appears later with comparable specificity.

```html
<style>
  :root { --space-unit: 0.5rem; }
  /* These utility rules could be generated by a Sass loop at build time. */
  .gap-1 { gap: calc(var(--space-unit) * 1); }
  .gap-2 { gap: calc(var(--space-unit) * 2); }
  .gap-3 { gap: calc(var(--space-unit) * 3); }
  .compact { --space-unit: 0.25rem; }
</style>
<div class="compact gap-3">Compiled selector, runtime spacing token</div>
```
Sass can generate the utility selectors; --space-unit remains live in the output so a compact container can change their final gaps.
